<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RQFlinch - shot while already down, compressed. Back half of the pair; see
  RQFlinchFront.xml beside it, and hitreaction/RQFlinch.xml for the design.

  WHY THIS STATE MATTERS, and why the TODO entry that asked for it had the
  reason wrong. It said "shot while down, reaction Floor". HitReaction.FLOOR is
  never produced by gunfire - resolveHitReaction cannot return it
  (CombatManager.java:2313-2377), and the only writers are a vehicle impact on
  a prone or getting-up character (IsoGameCharacter.java:11643) and bent fences
  (BentFences.java:362,370). The reaction-string route
  (actiongroups/zombie/onground/to_hitreaction-onfloor.xml, which compares
  hitreaction == "Floor") is therefore the VEHICLE lane.

  The gunfire lane is the other doorway, and it needs no particular string:
  getup/, getup-fromSitting/ and getup-fromOnBack/to_hitreaction-onfloor.xml
  each carry a transition whose whole condition is <isTrue>hashitreaction</isTrue>
  plus <isTrue>bOnFloor</isTrue>. Our own compressed crit chain routes through
  getup-fromOnBack with bOnFloor still true until SetOnFloor(false) fires at
  the halfway mark, so a second shot inside that window lands here.

  WHAT IT COSTS UNCOVERED: vanilla's FloorBack runs at m_SpeedScale 0.96 and
  emits KnockDown, which is setOnFloor(true) AND setKnockedDown(true)
  (ZombieHitReactionState.java:106-109) - so an uncovered hit here both extends
  the floor trip by a full animation and re-arms the knockdown flag on a
  zombie the feature had just picked up.

  Exit is <eventOccurred>ActiveAnimFinishing</eventOccurred> -> onground
  (actiongroups/zombie/hitreaction-onfloor/to_onground.xml), so speed scaling
  genuinely shortens it, same as every other node in this family.

  Copied from vanilla FloorBack.xml. Changed only by: our condition added
  alongside vanilla's FallOnFront one, an explicit priority, speed 20, zeroed
  blend, and KnockDown swapped for CancelKnockDown. Collide and SetState are
  vanilla's, kept verbatim.

  DROPPING KnockDown DOES NOT LEAVE bOnFloor UNSET. KnockDown's other half is
  setOnFloor(true), but bOnFloor was already true - it is a condition of every
  transition that reaches this state - and nothing clears it here; the exit
  into onground sets it again anyway (ZombieOnGroundState.java:41-43). So there
  is no SetOnFloor event to add back.

  TWO NODES, NOT ONE, because vanilla splits on FallOnFront and that flag is
  what routes onground into the matching getup. A single node winning both
  orientations would play one clip for both, which is invisible at 20x - but it
  would also mean inferring that the flag survives from the original fall
  rather than letting vanilla assert it. The split costs one file and removes
  the inference.
-->
<animNode>
	<m_Name>RQFlinchFloorBack</m_Name>
	<m_AnimName>Zombie_HitReact_FloorOnBack</m_AnimName>
	<m_Looped>false</m_Looped>
	<m_BlendTime>0.0</m_BlendTime>
	<m_BlendOutTime>0.0</m_BlendOutTime>
	<m_SpeedScale>20.00</m_SpeedScale>
	<m_ConditionPriority>100</m_ConditionPriority>
	<m_Conditions x_name="rqf1a2b3-6001-4000-8000-rftddirge6001">
		<m_Name>RQNoFlinch</m_Name>
		<m_Type>BOOL</m_Type>
		<m_Value>true</m_Value>
	</m_Conditions>
	<m_Conditions x_name="rqf1a2b3-6002-4000-8000-rftddirge6002">
		<m_Name>FallOnFront</m_Name>
		<m_Type>BOOL</m_Type>
		<m_Value>false</m_Value>
	</m_Conditions>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-6003-4000-8000-rftddirge6003">
		<m_EventName>CancelKnockDown</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue>true</m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-6004-4000-8000-rftddirge6004">
		<m_EventName>Collide</m_EventName>
		<m_TimePc>0.1</m_TimePc>
		<m_ParameterValue></m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-6005-4000-8000-rftddirge6005">
		<m_EventName>SetState</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue></m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
</animNode>
